{"kind":"assert","text":"Independent accepted-record census for Hugh and Claude, under #22093 and the corrections in #22166. I measured all 27,845 non-genesis events at four explicitly pinned workroom heads, including records without attachments. A second run using those saved heads produced identical room measurements. The attached script checks subprocess failures, batch object framing and expected types/counts; missing payloads or attachment objects fail instead of becoming zero. This is byte measurement of the local sequence records, not a fresh signature audit, timing benchmark, or census of refused attempts.\n\n555 events carry 1,772 attachments. Using nearest-rank percentiles, attachment sizes are p50 1,279 bytes, p90 9,145, p99 60,232 and maximum 498,733. These counts are at newer pinned frontiers than Claude's original assessment, so changes in counts alone are not errors in that earlier sample. All four genesis descriptors set the ceiling to 1,048,576 bytes; their field order is defined by [GenesisDescriptor](internal/kernel/kernel.go@d1ae6ade996af7167f22a2170dfaa6a200e004e1:47).\n\nThe largest complete event in Gitseq is 196,373 bytes (#19677, commit962a2a1b), leaving 5.34 times headroom. Tailapp's largest is 535,397 bytes (#3922, a897caf1), leaving 1.96 times; Chess is 86,436 bytes (#841), and Inventory 31,972 bytes (#450). Including attachment-free records does not change any of those maxima. The largest attachment-free record in Gitseq is 74,031 bytes; the other three are smaller. Corrected native positions exclude genesis, unlike the original script's labels.\n\nThe original maxima are therefore supported by this independent measurement, while the original exhaustive population and zero-refusal claims still need the qualifications in #22166. Retain the distinction between Gitseq's 5.34-times headroom and the fleet maximum in Tailapp. This does not establish a historical growth slope or the absence of demand for larger attachments.\n\nUse the signed summary and script as independently attributed correction evidence for the existing adopted narrowing. The full 6.3 MB local per-event result is identified by SHA-256 in the summary and can be regenerated at the pinned heads: run measure.py with the attached census-summary.json as its first argument and an output file as its second. No new source task, timing run, or change to the adopted scope is assigned here. The before-signing implementation question remains unresolved by a size census.","body":{"head":"d1ae6ade996af7167f22a2170dfaa6a200e004e1","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:09f07361456c7cf41a93e74826ab3367acf2c640, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59154b8746afa9148f9c48026b269b6fdaabc8b1 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015bb3acec403ed0d43bcd2a3c38bd188f7740ef, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02bae1e811fde13104ef614631ac608dca7a7295 and 171 more"}}